/** @page ai_model_deployment AI Model Deployment and ONNX Inference

    @tableofcontents

    @section ai_model_deployment_overview Overview

    %Qore supports production model execution with ONNX Runtime, typed tensor
    values, model package metadata, registry-managed artifacts, and
    DataProvider-aware pipeline processors. The focus is inference and
    deployment: models are normally trained or exported by existing ML tooling,
    then inspected, packaged, validated, benchmarked, registered, and executed
    by %Qore.

    The main pieces are:
    - the @ref mlintro "ml" module, which exposes @ref Qore::ML::OnnxModel
      "ML::OnnxModel", @ref Qore::ML::Tensor "ML::Tensor",
      @ref Qore::ML::OnnxSessionPool "ML::OnnxSessionPool", provider
      diagnostics, profiling, graph optimization, ORT format loading, and
      typed tensor execution;
    - @ref qoreonnxtoolsintro "QoreOnnxTools" and the \c qore-onnx command,
      which inspect, validate, benchmark, compare, optimize, quantize, and
      package ONNX artifacts;
    - @ref qoremodelregistryintro "QoreModelRegistry", which stores native Qore
      models and multi-file ONNX packages with checksums, tokenizer companions,
      provider policy, execution plans, optimized/quantized artifacts, and
      deployment-contract metadata;
    - @ref dataprovidermlintro "DataProviderML", which runs registry-backed or
      path-backed ONNX models inside DataProvider pipelines with row, columnar,
      buffer-column, and DataFrame input shapes.

    @section ai_model_direct Direct ONNX Execution

    Use direct @ref Qore::ML::OnnxModel "ML::OnnxModel" APIs when the
    application already owns the model path, session policy, and input tensor
    preparation. Pass dense @ref Qore::ML::Tensor "ML::Tensor" values for
    predictable type and shape behavior.

    @code{.py}
%requires ml

hash<ML::OnnxSessionConfig> session = <ML::OnnxSessionConfig>{
    # Empty providers means explicit CPU-only execution; no GPU auto-detection.
    "providers": (),
    "intra_op_num_threads": 4,
    "enable_profiling": True,
};

ML::OnnxModel model("/models/customer-score.onnx", session);

ML::Tensor features(data: new buffer<float32>((
    41.0, 10000.0, 0.70,
    55.0,  5000.0, 0.20,
)), shape: (2, 3), dtype: "float32");

hash<auto> outputs = model.runTensors({"X": features});
printf("provider=%s runs=%d\n",
    model.getEffectiveProviderReport().active_provider,
    model.getInferenceStats().run_count);

string profile_path = model.endProfiling();
    @endcode

    For high-throughput services, use a bounded session pool. A pool keeps a
    fixed number of immutable ONNX sessions and applies backpressure instead of
    creating sessions on demand.

    @code{.py}
%requires ml

ML::OnnxSessionPool pool(
    "/models/customer-score.onnx",
    <ML::OnnxSessionConfig>{"providers": ()},
    {
        "max_sessions": 4,
        "max_concurrent_runs": 8,
        "queue_depth": 64,
        "timeout_ms": 1000,
        "dynamic_batch_size": 32,
        "dynamic_batch_wait_ms": 2,
    });

Future f = pool.runAsync({"X": features});
hash<auto> scored = f.get();
printf("queued=%d completed=%d\n",
    pool.getStats().queued_runs,
    pool.getStats().completed_runs);
    @endcode

    @section ai_model_provider_policy Provider Policy and Fallback

    ONNX Runtime providers are deployment policy, not an implementation detail.
    %Qore exposes both the requested provider policy and the effective provider
    report so production code can reject accidental CPU fallback or record it as
    a deployment diagnostic.

    - Omit \c providers to let %Qore try the best available accelerator provider
      reported by ONNX Runtime, then fall back to CPU when policy allows it.
    - Use \c providers: \c () for explicit CPU-only execution.
    - Set \c required_providers when a model version is only valid with a
      particular provider.
    - Set \c allow_cpu_fallback: \c False or
      \c fail_on_provider_fallback: \c True when silent fallback would be a
      deployment error.

    @code{.py}
hash<ML::OnnxSessionConfig> gpu_only = <ML::OnnxSessionConfig>{
    "providers": (<ML::OnnxProviderConfig>{
        "name": "CUDA",
        "options": {"device_id": "0"},
    },),
    "required_providers": ("CUDA",),
    "allow_cpu_fallback": False,
    "fail_on_provider_fallback": True,
};

ML::OnnxModel model("/models/reranker.onnx", gpu_only);
hash<auto> report = model.getEffectiveProviderReport();
printf("active=%s diagnostics=%y\n",
    report.active_provider, report.diagnostics);
    @endcode

    Migration note: code that previously relied on implicit provider selection
    should record \c getEffectiveProviderReport() and choose an explicit policy
    before moving to production. Use \c providers: \c () for reproducible
    CPU-only behavior in tests and CI.

    @section ai_model_package_registry Packaging and Registry Metadata

    ONNX deployments are often multi-file packages: a model file, external-data
    files, tokenizer artifacts, config files, optimization outputs, benchmark
    metadata, and validation vectors. The \c qore-onnx package command writes a
    deterministic package manifest that the model registry can ingest.

    @code{.sh}
qore-onnx package --task regression --opset 17 \
    --minimum-onnxruntime-version 1.16.0 \
    --preprocess preprocess-contract.json \
    --postprocess postprocess-contract.json \
    --dynamic-axes dynamic-axes.json \
    --benchmarks benchmarks.json \
    --golden-vectors golden-vectors.json \
    /models/customer-score.onnx /srv/model-packages/customer-score
    @endcode

    Register the package once, then load, inspect, validate, benchmark, compare,
    and execute it by model/version identity instead of by loose filesystem
    paths.

    @code{.py}
%requires QoreModelRegistry
%requires QoreOnnxTools
%requires ml

QoreModelRegistry::ModelRegistry reg({"path": "/var/models"});

string vid = reg.registerPackage("/srv/model-packages/customer-score", {
    "name": "customer-score",
    "description": "Customer scoring model exported to ONNX",
    "tags": ("production",),
    "task": "regression",
    "execution_config": <ML::OnnxSessionConfig>{"providers": ()},
});

hash<QoreModelRegistry::ModelVersionInfo> manifest =
    reg.getVersionManifest("customer-score", vid);
printf("task=%s opset=%y provider=%s valid=%y\n",
    manifest.task,
    manifest.opset,
    manifest.providers.active_provider,
    manifest.validation.valid);

hash<auto> inspection = reg.inspectOnnxVersion("customer-score", vid);
hash<QoreOnnxTools::OnnxValidationResult> validation =
    reg.validateOnnxVersion("customer-score", vid);
hash<QoreOnnxTools::OnnxBenchmarkResult> benchmark =
    reg.benchmarkOnnxVersion("customer-score", vid, NOTHING, {
        "runs": 100,
        "warmup_runs": 5,
    });
printf("inputs=%y valid=%y average=%fms\n",
    inspection.inputs, validation.valid, benchmark.average_ms);
    @endcode

    The registry stores the complete ONNX deployment contract at version level:
    \c task, \c opset, producer metadata,
    \c minimum_onnxruntime_version, input/output tensor contracts,
    \c dynamic_axes, \c external_data, tokenizer companions, preprocessing and
    postprocessing metadata, execution plan and session policy, provider
    diagnostics, optimized and quantized artifacts, validation diagnostics,
    benchmark metadata, and golden vectors.

    @section ai_model_dataprovider DataProviderML and DataFrames

    Use @ref dataprovidermlintro "DataProviderML" when ONNX inference is one
    step in a larger data pipeline. Processors accept row records, hash-of-lists
    data, dense buffer columns, and @ref dataframeintro "DataFrame" blocks.
    Shape-aware paths keep data columnar and avoid per-row hash materialization
    when the processor and downstream steps support it.

    @code{.py}
%requires DataProviderML
%requires dataframe

DataProviderML::QoreRegistryModelProcessor proc({
    "registry_path": "/var/models",
    "model_name": "customer-score",
    "tag": "production",
    "fields": ("age", "balance", "risk_score"),
    "window_size": 1024,
    "output_mode": "columns",
    "include_input_fields": False,
});

DataFrame::DataFrame batch({
    "age": new buffer<float64>((41.0, 55.0)),
    "balance": new buffer<float64>((10000.0, 5000.0)),
    "risk_score": new buffer<float64>((0.70, 0.20)),
});

DataFrame::DataFrame scored = proc.processRecordsShape(
    batch, DataProviderShape::DataFrameBlock);
printf("rows=%d columns=%y\n", scored.rowCount(), scored.columnNames());
    @endcode

    For transformer-style models with multiple named inputs, use \c input_map
    instead of relying on a single merged feature matrix.

    @code{.py}
DataProviderML::QoreRegistryModelProcessor classifier({
    "registry_path": "/var/models",
    "model_name": "text-classifier",
    "tag": "production",
    "input_map": {
        "input_ids": "token_ids",
        "attention_mask": "attention_mask",
        "token_type_ids": "segment_ids",
    },
    "output_mode": "columns",
});
    @endcode

    Registry-backed task plans provide stable outputs for common inference
    tasks. \c regression, \c classification, and
    \c sequence-classification plans emit task fields such as \c value,
    \c label, and \c scores while preserving raw ONNX outputs. \c embedding and
    \c reranker packages can be used by \c embed-text and \c rerank-text
    processors without duplicating mode options. Initial \c causal-lm plans
    support tokenizer loading, \c input_ids and \c attention_mask tensor
    construction, greedy decoding, stop tokens, generated-token metadata, and
    truthful reporting when stateful KV-cache reuse is not available.

    @section ai_model_optimization Optimization, Quantization, and Comparison

    Optimize, quantize, validate, compare, and benchmark model variants before
    promotion. %Qore records the variant lineage and provider compatibility
    metadata so callers do not accidentally load a hardware-specific ORT
    artifact with the wrong provider policy.

    @code{.py}
hash<QoreModelRegistry::ModelArtifactInfo> opt =
    reg.registerOptimizedOnnxArtifact("customer-score", vid, "cpu.ort", {
        "ort_format": True,
        "session_config": <ML::OnnxSessionConfig>{"providers": ()},
    });

hash<QoreModelRegistry::ModelArtifactInfo> int8 =
    reg.registerQuantizedOnnxArtifact("customer-score", vid, "int8.onnx", {
        "method": "dynamic",
        "weight_type": "QInt8",
        "compare": True,
        "benchmark": True,
        "runs": 100,
    });

hash<QoreOnnxTools::OnnxCompareResult> cmp =
    reg.compareOnnxVersionArtifacts(
        "customer-score", vid, opt.name, int8.name, {
            "abs_tolerance": 0.01,
            "rel_tolerance": 0.01,
            "benchmark": True,
        });
printf("match=%y speedup=%y\n", cmp.outputs_match, cmp.speedup);
    @endcode

    @section ai_model_benchmarks Benchmarking

    The source tree includes benchmark cases for raw ONNX tensor execution and
    DataProviderML ONNX pipelines. A focused local run can be started with:

    @code{.sh}
QORE_BENCH_ONNX_ROWS=10000 \
    bench/run.sh --filter='onnx|dataproviderml_onnx' \
    --iterations=5 --warmup=1 --save=/tmp/qore-onnx-bench.json
    @endcode

    Benchmark representative deployment shapes: row events, DataFrame blocks,
    buffer columns, single-input tensors, multi-input tensors, registry-loaded
    packages, optimized artifacts, and quantized artifacts. Measure with the
    same provider policy and thread settings that production will use.

    @section ai_model_limits Current Limits

    %Qore intentionally does not reimplement training frameworks or ONNX
    exporters. Use the exporter that best fits the source model, then use
    %Qore for packaging, deployment metadata, validation, benchmarking, and
    runtime execution.

    Current implementation boundaries:
    - half-precision and unsigned ONNX tensor outputs can be exposed through
      wider Qore buffer types when there is no exact Qore dense buffer element
      type;
    - provider-owned device buffers are not exposed as portable Qore values;
    - DataProviderML \c causal-lm execution performs greedy decoding and can
      reuse ONNX present-key/value tensors as past-key/value inputs when the
      model exposes matching cache tensor metadata; unsupported cache layouts
      are reported in diagnostics;
    - external quantization and conversion workflows depend on the installed
      Python ONNX Runtime tooling; \c qore-onnx check-python reports missing
      dependencies and versions.
*/
